    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change follows the codebase's established conventions cleanly:\n\n- **Mapper method placement is idiomatic.** `formatTelephoneDisplay` sits alongside the other `formatX` default methods in `OwnerMapper`, uses the identical `@Named` + `qualifiedByName` wiring, the same null-owner guard, and delegates out rather than inlining logic \u2014 exactly matching `formatDisplayName`, `formatMembershipNumber`, `normalizeTelephone`, etc. A maintainer would find it in the expected spot.\n\n- **The formatting logic lives in the right unit.** `TelephoneNormalizer` is already the domain home for E.164 telephone knowledge (country-code lengths, digit stripping) and is already the class `OwnerMapper` delegates telephone concerns to via `toE164`. `toDisplay` is the natural inverse operation and belongs with that knowledge; introducing a separate one-method `TelephoneFormatter` would be over-engineering here. The private `countryCodeLength` helper keeps `toDisplay` readable and mirrors the existing `hasValidNationalLength` helper style.\n\n- **No harmful duplication.** `countryCodeLength` and `hasValidNationalLength` both branch on `\"61\"`/`\"1\"`, but they answer different questions (cc length vs. national-length validity) and factoring them together would couple two unrelated rules; leaving them separate is the more coherent choice.\n\n- **OpenAPI addition is consistent** with the surrounding read-only derived fields (`initials`, etc.): `readOnly: true`, title/description/example present.\n\nOne minor, non-blocking observation: `TelephoneNormalizer`'s class-level Javadoc still describes it purely as normalizing input to E.164, so a display formatter mildly stretches its stated scope \u2014 but the class already carries `isValid` as a mixed-but-cohesive telephone concern, so this stays within the class's evident responsibility and isn't worth requesting a change over.",
